# Workers Static Assets reads this from the deployed asset directory (Vite copies
# public/ into dist/ verbatim, and os/wrangler.jsonc serves ./dist).

# ARENA ENTRIES ARE MODEL-WRITTEN CODE.
#
# os.mino.mobi is inside the `.mino.mobi` SSO cookie scope and its localStorage
# can hold an Anthropic API key. A bake-off entry is HTML written by whichever
# model was on trial — it is not hostile, but it is not reviewed line by line
# either, and it must never be able to reach either of those.
#
# `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox allow-scripts` puts the document in an
# OPAQUE ORIGIN: it may run scripts (the entries are WebGPU games, so they must)
# but it is same-origin with nothing, so document.cookie is empty and
# localStorage/sessionStorage throw. The arena page additionally frames each
# entry with sandbox="allow-scripts" — this header is what covers the case the
# iframe attribute cannot, namely someone opening an entry URL directly.
#
# allow-scripts WITHOUT allow-same-origin is the load-bearing combination.
# Adding allow-same-origin would hand the sandbox its origin back and undo all
# of this.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT WORK. Measured twice on the live site — at
# compatibility_date 2024-09-23 and again after bumping to 2026-02-20 and
# redeploying — the header is absent from responses under /arena/entries/.
# Workers Static Assets is not honouring `_headers` for this worker, and the
# compatibility date was not the cause. Re-check any time with:
#
#   curl -sSI https://os.mino.mobi/arena/race-01/entries/<cell>/ | grep -i content-security
#
# THE FIX, when someone wants it: give `os` a small `main` worker script with an
# assets binding (`assets.binding` in wrangler.jsonc) that fetches the asset and
# adds the header itself. That is a change to a working deploy's shape and needs
# the golden rule re-verified after it, so it has not been done on spec.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY PROTECTS ENTRIES TODAY: the arena's play pages
# (os/public/arena/<run>/play/<cell>/, built by bakeoff/arena.mjs) frame every
# entry with sandbox="allow-scripts allow-pointer-lock" and NO
# allow-same-origin. That is a confirmed opaque origin, and it is the normal way
# in. What remains unprotected is somebody navigating straight to an entry URL
# — the "open raw" link, or a hand-typed path.
#
# The rules below are kept because they are correct and cost nothing if support
# ever lands.
/arena/entries/*
  Content-Security-Policy: sandbox allow-scripts
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
